+// ---- problem/rt_kappa_hsmooth: THE SWEEP'S EXTINCTION, SMEARED SIDEWAYS -------------
+// THE DEFECT IT IS FOR.  The radial transport here is a per-column two-stream: each
+// (j,k) column is integrated with its OWN chi = kappa rho and nothing crosses between
+// columns.  Once convection makes rho strongly inhomogeneous at nearly uniform T (rms
+// rho/<rho> 0.4-0.9, T uniform to ~1 %, He4 wedge past ~2 turnovers) the per-column
+// flux goes as 1/chi, so the shell average of it is <1/chi> and not 1/<chi>: the
+// POROSITY factor <1/chi><chi> = 1.1-1.5 (tests_r19/r21/r24/r26/r27), L_out reaches
+// 1.2-1.3 L and the envelope drains.  It is a real effect only if the low-chi channels
+// are wider than a photon mean free path.  They are not: the channels have
+// autocorrelation lengths 5-14e9 cm and the mean free path inside them is 0.13-0.85 of
+// that (tests_r21/out_chan.txt), so a photon leaving a channel wanders into the
+// neighbouring clumps well before it escapes -- which a strictly radial sweep cannot
+// represent, and which is why the channel flux is overestimated.
+//
+// WHAT THIS DOES.  Before the sweep, chi is replaced by its HORIZONTAL average over a
+// neighbourhood of radius fac x the local photon mean free path l = 1/chi, measured in
+// physical ARC LENGTH (r dtheta, r sin(theta) dphi -- the DX2/DX3 of this file), and
+// kc_g is set back to chi_smoothed/rho.  kc_g is the ONE opacity array the whole solver
+// reads, so the layer optical depths, the BFace emissivity weighting, Em, the mode-3
+// column solve and the radiative force all see the same smeared extinction and the
+// sweep remains exactly a two-stream with extinction chi_s and source function B(T).
+// NEITHER the density that carries the heat capacity NOR the temperature the emissivity
+// is built from is touched, and the deposit is still a per-column flux difference, so
+// the energy bookkeeping is unchanged: only the fluxes move.
+//
+// ARITHMETIC, NOT HARMONIC.  A ray leaving a channel at a small angle to the radial
+// direction crosses the neighbouring clumps within one mean free path, so the
+// extinction it integrates is the LINEAR average of chi over that neighbourhood.  The
+// harmonic mean 1/<1/chi> is, by construction, what the strictly radial per-column
+// sweep already produces when its columns are averaged -- i.e. exactly the porosity
+// this option exists to remove -- so averaging chi harmonically would be a no-op.
+//
+// THE WEIGHT is a tent, w = 1 - d/R, in the transverse distance d, normalised over the
+// valid neighbours it reaches, with R = fac/chi capped at rt_khs_ncap cells.  Where
+// l is below one cell no neighbour can carry weight, and the cell is then left BITWISE
+// alone (the early return below), so the optically-thick deep interior -- the diffusion
+// limit -- is untouched by construction.  Where l >> dx the stencil saturates at the
+// cap.  Grey split path only; default off and then no kernel runs at all.
+inline bool rt_kappa_hsmooth = false;
+inline Real rt_kappa_hsmooth_fac = 1.0;
+// how many cells the stencil may reach, capped again by nghost: chi is rebuilt in the
+// angular ghosts (see the pre-kernel ranges) so that a MeshBlock edge is bit for bit a
+// single-block run, and the ghosts are only nghost deep.
+inline constexpr int rt_khs_ncap = 3;
+inline DvceArray4D<Real> *rt_khs_ptr = nullptr;   // (m,k,j,i) chi = kappa rho [1/cm]
+
